caption: Classes and Functions — Overview
technology-rule: overview

Define what the modification is before deciding how much it costs.

Cybernetics include hard bionics, artificial organs, neural interfaces, implanted computers, biotech replacements, living metal, magitech grafts, and full machine bodies. The source changes repair and vulnerability; the GURPS traits define reliable capability.

The framework is setting-neutral. Use the same build logic whether the system comes from a corporate clinic, battlefield workshop, divine forge, or nanofactory.

| Design Question | Example Answers |
| --- | --- |
| What does it grant? | Arm ST, DR, Night Vision, Resistant, Flight, Payload |
| What does it replace? | A lost limb, damaged eye, failing organ, biological body |
| What disables it? | EMP, power loss, hacking, anti-magic, disease, corruption |
| Who repairs it? | Cyberclinic, robotics bay, biotech lab, divine forge, artificer |
| What does society call it? | Medicine, military hardware, golemcraft, miracle, curse |

| Society | Common Interpretation |
| --- | --- |
| High-cyber city | Medicine, fashion, professional equipment, corporate class. |
| Military state | Licensed hardware, service marker, controlled equipment. |
| Low-tech fantasy realm | Miracle, curse, golemcraft, demonwork, or divine sign. |
| Cultivation society | External strength unless harmonized with cultivation. |
| Post-apocalyptic mercenary state | Power purchased through sacrifice, debt, or lost humanity. |
| Humanitarian reconstruction facility | Ethical medicine when chosen, dignified, and protective. |

REPLACE BEFORE YOU STACK

Price the net improvement. A replacement system normally substitutes for the natural function before added advantages stack.

PLAYER PROMISE

A paid trait works reliably under normal conditions. Only listed, meaningful limitations should disable it.
